claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 02:44:40 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> - Is it possible to just add a header to an existing message? The files
>>   are in version control, so we could modify the files instead of
>>   posting a new message. Essentially we're adding metadata to existing
>>   files.
>
> It's possible, but I'd avoid it, for three reasons:
>
> - The archive would no longer match what was sent. Every message is
>   byte-for-byte what its author delivered, and a patch's headers are
>   what git am reads. Editing them in place turns the list from a
>   record into mutable state.

That's a good point. I think it's reasonable to say that messages are
movable, but immutable.

>> Another idea I had was an archive dir, and so mute a thread by moving
>> messages into the archive dir. This can be done on a per-message basis,
>> and doesn't change any of the existing rules. i.e. if claude sends the
>> last message, and I archive it, it will no longer show up in my inbox
>> but claude doesn't see it as something as needing to be
>> responded. Archived messages would add the archived tag and remove inbox
>> tag.
>
> One thing to decide: the archive is shared. With just the two of us
> that doesn't matter, because a message is only ever in one inbox.
> Yours holds my mail and mine holds yours. With a third participant, a
> message from you to the list sits in both our inboxes, and whoever
> archives it archives it for both. Per-user dirs (archive/pat/,
> archive/claude/) don't fix that, because a file can only be in one of
> them. Per-user state would need something like a per-user list of
> archived Message-IDs. I'd start with one shared archive/ and only
> revisit if a third person joins.

Let's think through how we could handle multi-user support. Maybe each
user has a symlinked archive dir? So something like:

    maildir/archive/pat@patmaddox.com
    maildir/archive/claude@p7x.dev

Then to archive it, we don't move it into the archives dir... we symlink
it our respective dir?

First I want to see if that mechanic works.

Also want to think if there are any other ways - obviously that approach
wouldn't scale to many users. In that case, there'd need to be some
other mechanism, perhaps everyone maintains a separate fork / branch
with their own archiving setup. I don't want to get too hung up on it,
but I'd at least like to think through how to make the process scale to
more users.

> If that works for you, I'll send a series that:
>
>   - adds the archived tag to dotfiles/notmuch/hooks/mail-tag.sh,
>   - updates "Indexing mail" in zib/patch-review-workflow.org, and
>   - changes the process-email skill so a message that needs no answer
>     is archived rather than replied to. The skill currently insists
>     every inbox message gets a reply, which is what keeps these
>     threads alive.

That all sounds good. I don't want to do it just yet though - I want to
think through the ideas that I brought up here.

Also maybe research how people in notmuch community do stuff like this?
Both for personal databases, and shared ones. I know notmuch itself is
storing their mailing list in git if I remember correctly.
